        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "If I wanted to use Tox on a system with 1st party keyloggers and as backdoored as Windows 8+, I'd be using Discord, because then there is **zero** point to using qTox, or any other Tox client.\n\nWhile Windows as a whole is not ideal (nor is GNU/Linux, BSD etc., for the gullible fools out there who might think otherwise), there's a MASSIVE divide in privacy violation in the jump between Windows 7 SP1 (which must also ignore certain updates) and **any** Windows version that came after it.\n\nThe original qTox, from the original authors and effort from its own official repo, never had this issue, all the way up to its final version, 1.17.6, from as late as March 2022.\n\nBut suddenly, with this fork and so-called \"1.18.0\", it 100% needlessly and pointlessly destroys that compatibility for absolutely no technical reason whatsoever, although excuses are _guaranteed_, I'm sure (else this decision wouldn't have been made and no one would have had a problem in the first place, and this issue would not exist).\n\nI appreciate anyone's desire to continue a Tox client at all, but if this is what you are going to do, it causes **more harm than not doing anything at all**, because when you do this, it creates the illusion that the situation of Tox for Windows is being taken care of by _someone_, thus sabotaging the effort, since then there are lower chances of someone understanding the situation, then chiming in to help.\n\nAll in all, this is my way of saying: don't let Microsoft, nor any other entity for that matter, decide what system is to be used or not to be used, and also that compatibility is KING in this already-thoroughly-fragmented software ecosystem.\n\nThank you.",
